# Code Review and Merge Workflow

## Goal

Define the end-to-end lifecycle from writeup through implementation to code review and merge, including all the git-meta keys needed to store review state. This closes the loop on ticgit's workflow: ideas become tickets, tickets become branches, branches get reviewed, and reviews get merged.

## The Full Lifecycle

```
writeup → ticket → sub-issues + specs → implement → code link → review → approve → merge → close
```

### Phase 1: Writeup (already implemented)

An idea starts as a writeup with versioned content.

```
target: project
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:title           (string)
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:status          (string)  open | closed
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:versions        (list)    versioned markdown content
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:authors         (set)     contributor emails
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:tags            (set)
ticgit:writeups:<uuid>:tickets         (set)     linked ticket UUIDs
```

Commands: `ti writeup new`, `ti writeup edit`, `ti writeup link`

### Phase 2: Ticket + Sub-issues + Spec (already implemented)

Promote a writeup to a ticket, break it down, add implementation specs.

```
target: project
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:title            (string)
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:spec             (string)  implementation specification
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:parent           (string)  parent ticket UUID
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:children         (set)     child ticket UUIDs
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:writeups         (set)     linked writeup UUIDs
```

Commands: `ti writeup promote`, `ti new --subissue`, `ti spec`

### Phase 3: Implementation + Code Link (already implemented)

Developer works on a branch, links it to the ticket.

```
target: project
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:code             (string)  https://host/repo:branch
```

Commands: `ti checkout`, `ti code`

### Phase 4: Review (NEW)

Reviews live on git-meta branch targets, outside the ticgit namespace. The branch itself is the review identity - no review UUID needed.

```
target: branch/<branch-id>
review:title            (string)   defaults to ticket title or branch name
review:status           (string)   open | approved | changes-requested | merged | closed
review:base             (string)   base branch name (e.g. main)
review:head             (string)   head commit SHA at time of review request
review:ticket           (string)   linked ticket UUID (optional)
review:reviewers        (set)      requested reviewer emails
review:approvals        (set)      emails who approved
review:comments         (list)     structured review comments (JSON)
review:revisions        (list)     review round markers
review:created-at       (string)   RFC3339
review:created-by       (string)   email
review:merged-at        (string)   RFC3339
review:merged-by        (string)   email
```

Backlink on the ticket (project target):
```
ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:reviews   (set)   branch IDs with active reviews
```

**Why branch targets?** The review IS about the branch. No artificial UUID needed. The data is portable - any tool can read `review:*` keys on a branch, not just ticgit. This also enables review workflows in repos that don't use ticgit's ticket system at all.

**Why outside the ticgit namespace?** Review metadata on branches is a general-purpose concept. Keeping it as `review:*` rather than `ticgit:review:*` makes it a shared convention that other tools can adopt.

## Review Comments Format

More structured than ticket comments - supports inline code comments:

```json
{
  "author": "alice@example.com",
  "body": "Needs bounds checking",
  "type": "comment",
  "commit": "abc123",
  "path": "src/parser.rs",
  "line": 42
}
```

Types: `comment`, `approval`, `changes-requested`, `resolved`

The `commit`, `path`, and `line` fields are optional - general review comments omit them, inline comments include them.

## Patch Queue / Revisions

Track what changed between review rounds:

```json
{"head": "abc123", "message": "addressed review feedback"}
```

Stored in the `revisions` list. Lets reviewers see "what changed since I last looked" by diffing between revision entries.

## Proposed Commands

```bash
# Create a review (branch-centric)
ti review new                                # current branch, base=main
ti review new --branch sc-caching --base main
ti review new --ticket a3f                   # links to ticket, uses ticket's code branch

# List reviews
ti review list
ti review list --status open

# Show review (defaults to current branch)
ti review show
ti review show sc-caching

# Add reviewers
ti review add-reviewer alice@example.com
ti review add-reviewer sc-caching alice@example.com

# Leave a review comment
ti review comment "looks good but check the edge case on line 42"
ti review comment --path src/parser.rs --line 42 "needs bounds checking"

# Approve / request changes
ti review approve
ti review approve sc-caching
ti review request-changes "needs error handling in the parser"

# Update head commit (after pushing new changes)
ti review update                             # updates head to current HEAD
ti review update --head abc123

# Merge
ti review merge                              # record merge event
ti review merge --execute                    # record + git merge
```

Most commands default to the current branch when no branch is specified.

## Full Lifecycle Example

```bash
# 1. Start with a writeup
ti writeup new --title "Add caching layer"
ti writeup edit 075

# 2. Promote to ticket
ti writeup promote 075 --tags feature

# 3. Break into sub-issues
ti new --title "Cache invalidation strategy" --subissue a3f
ti new --title "Redis adapter" --subissue a3f
ti new --title "Cache hit metrics" --subissue a3f

# 4. Add spec to parent ticket
ti spec a3f

# 5. Implement on a branch, link code
git checkout -b sc-caching
ti checkout a3f
ti state in-progress
ti code a3f https://github.com/org/repo:sc-caching

# 6. Create review (on the branch)
ti review new --ticket a3f --base main

# 7. Add reviewers
ti review add-reviewer alice@example.com

# 8. Reviewer leaves comments
ti review comment "Need to handle cache stampede"
ti review request-changes "See comment about stampede"

# 9. Author pushes fixes, updates review
git push
ti review update
ti review comment "Added stampede protection with singleflight"

# 10. Reviewer approves
ti review approve

# 11. Merge and close
ti review merge --execute
ti close a3f
```

## Open Questions

1. **Should `--execute` actually run `git merge`?** Or just record the event? Probably both options: default records only, `--execute` does the merge.

2. **GitHub/GitLab sync.** Should `ti review new` optionally create a GitHub PR? Should `ti import gh` import PR review state? Future concern, but the data model should support it.

3. **Review without a ticket.** Reviews should work standalone - quick fixes don't always need a ticket. The `review:ticket` field is optional.

4. **Agent integration.** `ti context` should include active review state. `ti review show --markdown` for agent-friendly output with next-step suggestions.

5. **Multiple reviews per ticket.** Supported naturally - different branches can each have review data, and the ticket's `reviews` set tracks all of them.

6. **Branch deletion.** What happens to review data when a branch is deleted? git-meta branch targets use a branch UUID internally, so the data persists even if the branch name is reused. But should we archive/snapshot the review state somewhere more permanent?